<div style="clear:both;"></div>

---

## JSON: What Data Looks Like

When apps receive data from an API, it’s often in a format called **JSON**.

<div style="background-color: #e4e2f8; border-radius: 10px; padding: 15px; margin: 20px auto; max-width: 85%; box-sizing: border-box; word-wrap: break-word; overflow-wrap: break-word;">
<p style="font-size: 12pt; margin-top: 12px; line-height: 120%;">
📖 <strong>JSON (JavaScript Object Notation)</strong> is a lightweight format for storing and transporting data, often returned by APIs.
</p>
</div>

JSON is designed to be:
- Easy for humans to read
- Easy for computers to generate

**Example (Weather Data):**

<div style="background-color: #f2f2f2; border-radius: 8px; padding: 16px; margin: 20px auto; max-width: 85%; font-family: monospace;">
<p style="font-size: 12pt; margin: 0; line-height: 1.4;">
{<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;"city": "Chicago",<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;"temperature": 72,<br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;"condition": "Cloudy"<br>
}
</p>
</div>


 <p style="font-size: 12pt;">
Look closely at the JSON example above. <strong>If an app only wanted to show the temperature</strong>, which part of the data would it need to look for?
<br><br></p>

<div style="clear:both;"></div>

<details style="background-color: #ececec; padding: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px; border: 1px solid transparent; border-radius: 5px;">
    <summary style="font-size: 12pt; font-weight: bold;">🔎 Check your thinking:</summary>
<div style="margin-top: 20px; margin-left: 20px; margin-right: 20px; margin-bottom:75px;"><p style="font-size: 12pt;">
In JSON, data is organized using <strong>key–value pairs</strong>. <br><br>The app can look for the key <strong>"temperature"</strong> and then use the value associated with it (<strong>72</strong>). <br><br>This structure makes it easier for apps to pull out just the specific pieces of data they need.
</p>
</div>
</details>


MARKDOWN
